=== layout: section

# Recombination

=== layout: one-panel

# *How Linkage Affects Inheritance*

@body

- Morgan did other experiments with fruit flies to see how linkage affects inheritance of two characters
- Morgan crossed flies that differed in traits of body color and wing size

=== layout: one-panel

@body

![True-breeding P cross produces wild-type F1 dihybrids](assets/lect08b-chap8-linked_genes/morgan_parental_cross.drawn.svg)

- Morgan mated true-breeding P (parental) generation flies to produce heterozygous F1 dihybrids all of which are wild-type in appearance.

=== layout: one-panel

# The F~1~ Testcross

@body

![F1 dihybrid female testcross with recessive male](assets/lect08b-chap8-linked_genes/morgan_testcross.drawn.svg)

- He then mated wild-type F1 dihybrid females with homozygous recessive males.
- This testcross will reveal the genotype of the eggs made by the dihybrid female.

=== layout: one-panel


@body

- Morgan found that body color and wing size are usually inherited together in specific combinations (parental phenotypes)
- He noted that these [*genes do not assort independently*]{underline=true}, and reasoned that they were on the same chromosome

=== layout: one-panel

@body

![Labeled diagram from source slide 23](assets/lect08b-chap8-linked_genes/morgan_most_offspring.drawn.svg)

=== layout: one-panel

@body

![Labeled diagram from source slide 24](assets/lect08b-chap8-linked_genes/morgan_testcross_offspring.drawn.svg)

=== layout: one-panel

@body

{table-style=comparison}
| PREDICTED RATIOS |  |  |  |  |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Genes on different chromosomes: | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 |
| Genes on the same chromosome: | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0 |
| [Results]{color=#FF6600} | 965 | 944 | 206 | 185 |

Both predictions are wrong...

=== layout: one-panel

# *Conclusion*

@body

- Since most offspring had a parental (P generation) phenotype, Morgan concluded that the genes for body color and wing size are genetically linked on the same chromosome.
- However, the production of a relatively small number of offspring with non-parental phenotypes indicated that some mechanism occasionally breaks the linkage between specific alleles of genes on the same chromosome.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Linked Genes and Crossing Over*

@body

- Morgan discovered that genes can be linked, but the linkage was incomplete, because some recombinant phenotypes were observed
- He proposed that some process must occasionally break the physical connection between genes on the same chromosome
- That mechanism was the [*crossing over*]{underline=true} of homologous chromosomes

=== layout: one-panel

# *Crossing over*

@body

- Takes place in prophase in meiosis I after chromosomes have duplicated
- Results in reciprocal exchange of equal and corresponding segments of homologous chromosomes
- Sites of crossing over are random along the length of chromosome pairs

