@left

![No crossing over: four parental products](assets/lect08b-chap8-linked_genes/no_crossover.drawn.svg)

@right

- (A) Alleles in the same chromosome remain together when there is no crossing over between them.

=== layout: two-panels

# *Crossing Over and Recombinant Gametes*

@left

![Single nonsister crossover: two parental and two recombinant products](assets/lect08b-chap8-linked_genes/single_crossover.drawn.svg)

@right

- (B) When there is crossing over, the result is two recombinant and two non-recombinant products, because the exchange is between only two of the four chromatids.

=== layout: definitions

# *Concepts in Genetic Linkage*

@body

- *Parental type* - allele configuration is the same as the parents in a cross
  - Cis combination in parents is observed in offspring
- *Recombinant type* &#8211; *non-parental type* - allele combinations different from those in the parental chromosomes
  - Cis combination in parents, but we observe in trans offspring
- *Syntenic* - refers to genes in the same chromosome regardless of whether they show independent assortment or linkage

